# ☁️ AWS Learning Journey — Hands-On Projects & Cloud Engineering

A collection of hands-on projects, experiments, and practical learnings focused on **Amazon Web Services (AWS), Python automation, serverless architecture, cloud deployment, monitoring, networking, and container orchestration**.

This repository documents my journey of learning AWS by building real-world applications and implementing cloud services rather than relying only on theoretical concepts.

## 🎯 Learning Objectives

- Understand core AWS services and cloud architecture.
- Build serverless applications using AWS Lambda and API Gateway.
- Store and manage files using Amazon S3.
- Integrate AI capabilities using Amazon Rekognition, Amazon Textract, and Amazon Polly.
- Automate cloud workflows using Python and Boto3.
- Work with databases using Amazon DynamoDB and Amazon RDS.
- Implement event-driven architectures using S3 events, EventBridge, and SNS.
- Deploy and scale applications using EC2, Load Balancers, Auto Scaling, Docker, ECS, and Kubernetes.
- Practice IAM permissions, security groups, monitoring, troubleshooting, and cloud resource management.

## 🛠️ AWS Services & Technologies

| Category | Services and Technologies |
|---|---|
| Compute | Amazon EC2, AWS Lambda |
| Storage | Amazon S3 |
| Databases | Amazon DynamoDB, Amazon RDS (MySQL) |
| Networking | Amazon API Gateway, Security Groups, VPC |
| AI & Machine Learning | Amazon Rekognition, Amazon Textract, Amazon Polly |
| Messaging & Events | Amazon SNS, Amazon EventBridge |
| Monitoring | Amazon CloudWatch |
| Identity & Security | AWS IAM, IAM Roles, IAM Policies |
| Containers | Docker, Amazon ECR, Amazon ECS, Kubernetes, Minikube |
| Automation | Python, Boto3 |
| Frontend | HTML, CSS, JavaScript |
| Backend | Python, Flask, REST APIs |
| Deployment | Nginx, Application Load Balancer, Network Load Balancer, Auto Scaling |

## 📚 Hands-On Projects

### 1. Happiest Face Detector

**Objective:** Build an application that analyzes facial expressions in uploaded or captured images.

**AWS services:** Amazon S3, AWS Lambda, Amazon Rekognition, Amazon DynamoDB, Amazon API Gateway.

**What I learned:**
- Uploading images to S3.
- Invoking Rekognition to detect facial attributes and emotions.
- Processing results through Lambda functions.
- Storing analysis results in DynamoDB.
- Connecting a web frontend to backend APIs.

### 2. Celebrity Recognition in Videos

**Objective:** Identify celebrities appearing in uploaded videos using Amazon Rekognition Video.

**AWS services:** Amazon S3, AWS Lambda, Amazon Rekognition, Amazon CloudWatch.

**What I learned:**
- Triggering Lambda functions from S3 upload events.
- Using asynchronous video-analysis operations.
- Working with celebrity-recognition results.
- Monitoring execution through CloudWatch logs.

### 3. Story to Speech

**Objective:** Convert written stories into audio using Amazon Polly.

**AWS services:** Amazon Polly, Amazon S3, AWS Lambda, Amazon API Gateway, Amazon DynamoDB.

**What I learned:**
- Generating speech from text.
- Saving generated MP3 files in S3.
- Building APIs for text-to-speech conversion.
- Storing and retrieving community story records.
- Understanding IAM permissions for database access.

### 4. Smart Civic Complaint System

**Objective:** Create a complaint-reporting workflow for civic problems such as garbage, traffic, streetlights, and potholes.

**AWS services:** Amazon S3, AWS Lambda, Amazon Rekognition, Amazon DynamoDB, Amazon API Gateway, Amazon EventBridge, Amazon SNS, Amazon Polly.

**What I learned:**
- Building an event-driven complaint-processing workflow.
- Capturing complaint details and location information.
- Classifying uploaded images.
- Storing complaints and their status in DynamoDB.
- Generating audio summaries and sending notifications.

### 5. Case File #001

**Objective:** Experiment with an AI-assisted image investigation workflow.

**AWS services:** Amazon S3, Amazon DynamoDB, AWS Lambda, Amazon Rekognition, Gemini API.

**What I learned:**
- Combining image analysis with generative AI.
- Passing structured data between cloud services.
- Building a backend workflow that combines AI-generated insights with image information.

### 6. AI Travel Memory

**Objective:** Build an AI-assisted travel-memory application using image analysis and generated content.

**AWS services:** Amazon S3, Amazon Rekognition, Amazon DynamoDB, Amazon Polly, Amazon SNS, AWS Lambda, Amazon API Gateway, Gemini API.

**What I learned:**
- Processing uploaded travel images.
- Combining image recognition with generative AI.
- Storing travel-related information.
- Generating audio and notifications.
- Integrating multiple AWS services into one application.

### 7. Order Management System

**Objective:** Develop a cloud-based order management application with order creation and tracking.

**AWS services:** Amazon S3, Amazon API Gateway, AWS Lambda, Amazon RDS (MySQL), Amazon SNS, Amazon EventBridge.

**What I learned:**
- Connecting serverless APIs to a relational database.
- Designing relational tables for orders and order items.
- Implementing order-processing workflows.
- Building dashboards and filtering records.
- Integrating event-driven notifications.

### 8. Serverless Image Resizer

**Objective:** Automatically generate resized image versions when a new image is uploaded.

**AWS services:** Amazon S3, AWS Lambda, AWS IAM, Amazon CloudWatch.

**Planned workflow:**

1. Upload an original image to S3.
2. Trigger Lambda through an S3 object-created event.
3. Process the image using Python.
4. Generate thumbnail, medium, and large versions.
5. Store resized images in a designated output location.

**Key learning areas:** Event-driven processing, IAM permissions, image processing, and CloudWatch troubleshooting.

### 9. Automate AWS Resource Provisioning Using Python

**Objective:** Learn to manage AWS resources programmatically instead of performing every operation manually through the AWS Console.

**Technologies:** Python, Boto3, AWS IAM, Amazon EC2, Amazon S3.

**Planned capabilities:**
- Create S3 buckets.
- Upload files to S3.
- Launch EC2 instances.
- Stop or terminate instances.
- Automate repetitive cloud-management tasks.

**Key learning areas:** AWS SDK usage, resource automation, permissions, and error handling.

### 10. Static Website Hosting on Amazon S3

**Objective:** Experiment with hosting static websites using S3 and a simple upload interface.

**Technologies:** Amazon S3, Python, Flask, HTML, CSS, JavaScript.

**Learning areas:**
- Uploading website files to S3.
- Organizing HTML, CSS, and JavaScript assets.
- Understanding static website hosting.
- Working with bucket policies and access permissions.
- Building a simple website-upload workflow.

### 11. Multi-Tier Web Application Deployment

**Objective:** Understand how a frontend, backend, and database can be separated into distinct application layers.

**AWS services:** Amazon EC2, Amazon S3, Amazon RDS, AWS IAM, Amazon VPC and Security Groups.

**What I learned:**
- Separating frontend and backend responsibilities.
- Connecting backend applications to a database.
- Understanding network access rules.
- Configuring database connectivity and environment variables.
- Exploring deployment options for multi-tier applications.

### 12. Scalable Web Application Using an Application Load Balancer

**Objective:** Distribute incoming HTTP traffic across EC2 instances and automatically replace failed or terminated instances.

**AWS services:** Amazon EC2, Application Load Balancer (ALB), Auto Scaling Groups, Target Groups, Security Groups, Amazon CloudWatch.

**What I learned:**
- Creating an AMI and launch template.
- Configuring an Auto Scaling Group.
- Registering instances with a target group.
- Monitoring target health.
- Testing traffic distribution.
- Observing automatic instance replacement after termination.

### 13. Scalable Web Application Using a Network Load Balancer

**Objective:** Explore load balancing for TCP-based workloads and understand how a Network Load Balancer differs from an Application Load Balancer.

**AWS services:** Amazon EC2, Network Load Balancer (NLB), Target Groups, Auto Scaling Groups, Security Groups.

**What I learned:**
- Configuring a Network Load Balancer.
- Registering targets and checking their health.
- Understanding listener protocols and ports.
- Comparing Layer 4 and Layer 7 load balancing.
- Troubleshooting unhealthy targets.

### 14. CloudOps Rescue Center

**Objective:** Build, containerize, deploy, scale, and recover a microservices application while learning Docker and Kubernetes.

**Technologies:** Python, Flask, Docker, Docker Compose, Amazon ECR, Amazon ECS (Fargate), Kubernetes, Minikube, kubectl, Amazon CloudWatch.

**Microservices:**
- `frontend` — incident management interface.
- `incident-api` — creates, retrieves, and updates incidents.
- `notification-service` — processes incident notifications.

**Progress and learning areas:**
- Creating separate Dockerfiles for each service.
- Building and running containers.
- Connecting services using Docker Compose networking.
- Managing configuration with environment variables.
- Pushing container images to Amazon ECR.
- Deploying services to Amazon ECS with Fargate.
- Configuring ECS task definitions and services.
- Debugging security groups and service-to-service connectivity.
- Creating Kubernetes Deployments, Pods, and Services.
- Exposing the Incident API through a NodePort Service.
- Connecting Kubernetes services using internal DNS names.
- Scaling the Incident API from one replica to three.
- Demonstrating automatic Pod replacement.
- Working with ConfigMaps and Secrets.

**Current status:** The ECS microservice flow and initial local Kubernetes deployments, service discovery, scaling, and self-healing experiments have been demonstrated. Remaining Kubernetes configuration and final documentation work can continue as the project progresses.

## 🧠 Key Concepts Practiced

### Serverless Architecture
- Lambda functions and triggers.
- API Gateway integrations.
- S3 event notifications.
- Event-driven application design.

### Storage and Databases
- S3 buckets, objects, and permissions.
- DynamoDB tables and records.
- Relational database connectivity using MySQL and RDS.

### AI Service Integration
- Facial attributes and emotion detection.
- Celebrity recognition in videos.
- Text-to-speech conversion.
- Document and image-processing workflows.
- Integrating external generative AI APIs.

### Networking and Deployment
- EC2 instances and AMIs.
- Security groups and port configuration.
- ALB and NLB.
- Target groups and health checks.
- Auto Scaling and instance recovery.
- Docker images, containers, and networking.
- ECS tasks and services.
- Kubernetes Pods, Deployments, Services, replicas, ConfigMaps, and Secrets.

### Monitoring and Troubleshooting
- Reading CloudWatch logs.
- Debugging API errors and failed requests.
- Investigating IAM permission issues.
- Diagnosing security-group and networking problems.
- Testing health endpoints.
- Understanding automatic recovery behavior.

